      "hints": [
        "The expected number of 4s in 30 throws is given by $\\mathrm{E}(X) = np = 30p$.",
        "Equate $30p$ to the experimental average $6.21$.",
        "Solve for $p = \\frac{6.21}{30}$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Equate the expected value $\\mathrm{E}(X) = np$ to the experimental average:\n$$30p = 6.21$$",
        "Solve for the single-throw probability $p$:\n$$p = \\frac{6.21}{30} = 0.207$$"
      ],
      "checking": {
        "mode": "ai",
        "ai_rubric": {
          "Constructing Sample Spaces": "Computes p = 6.21 / 30 = 0.207."
        },
        "criteria_source": "mark_scheme_structured"
      },
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "Let $X$ be the number of $4$s obtained in $n = 30$ independent throws of the biased die.\nThen $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(30, p)$, where $p$ is the probability of rolling a $4$ on any single throw.\nThe expected value is:\n$$\\mathrm{E}(X) = np = 30p$$\nWe are given that the long-run average is $6.21$:\n$$30p = 6.21 \\implies p = \\frac{6.21}{30} = 0.207$$"
      ]
    },
